ery room. People adopt these devices because they see a tangible benefit. And if you don’t trust an app’s privacy claims, you can choose devices that prioritize local processing, open-source firmware, or simple offline functionality. The *net* effect—across all users—is that those who value security and convenience get a powerful tool, while those who prioritize absolute solitude can simply decline. So yes, it’s a valid fear. But the evidence shows that, for most people, the ability to monitor their home, protect their family, and customize their privacy boundaries outweighs the theoretical risk—especially as security standards improve. The goal isn’t to put a microphone in every room; it’s to put *helpful* sensors where they’re needed, with your permission and control. That, I argue, is a net positive.
