ailures, we have better standards, more informed consumers, and a market that *rewards* strong privacy. The net effect is positive precisely because the technology is on a trajectory of improvement, not because it was born perfect. To call the whole thing a failure because it started badly would be like saying automobiles are a net negative because early models had no seatbelts and terrible brakes. They still got people where they needed to go—and then they got safer. So yes, the default was bad. But the *net*—the sum of benefits minus harms, across time and across users—has been and continues to be positive.
