oaring, and no way to know if your elderly mother has fallen until hours later. Those are real, measurable privacy and security failures. The question is not "Is IoT perfect?" but "Does IoT improve the overall safety and privacy landscape compared to not having it?" For most people, the answer is yes, even accounting for the bad actors and early flaws. Finally, **the hurt you mention was often concentrated and sensationalized, while the benefits were diffuse and quiet.** A hacked baby monitor makes national news; a smart CO detector saving a family of four from silent poisoning doesn't. That media asymmetry can make IoT seem far more dangerous than it actually is. When I look at the data—the millions of prevented break-ins, the thousands of medical alerts, the energy savings that reduce carbon emissions—the positive impact is undeniable. So the trade-off feels clear to me not because I dismiss the harms, but because I think we're often comparing an imagined perfect past (where privacy and security were flawless) against an imperfect present. That past never existed. Every technology—phones, email, credit cards—started insecure and got better. IoT is no different, and it's giving us real, measurable gains that are saving lives and protecting property right now, every day. That's the net positive.
